// EXPECTED: compile error
// expect-error: read-modify-writ
// BUG-806 (2026-08-17). Promoted from tests/zer_gaps/main_rmw_via_pointer_param.zer.
//
// main performs the read-modify-write through a HELPER and the ISR only stores. The
// ISR side of this family was closed by ISR-TRANS (which walks the interrupt body
// transitively); the MAIN side had no equivalent, so `g` was never recorded as
// compound-in-main and "shared between interrupt and main" never fired. main's read
// can be split from its store by the interrupt, silently clobbering the ISR's write —
// and on bare metal there is nothing to notice it by.
//
// Closed by a MEMOISED per-function RMW summary (bit n = performs an RMW through
// param n), queried at call sites. The obvious implementation — a transitive walk
// over every regular function body, mirroring ISR-TRANS — was implemented and
// REVERTED earlier: with the walker's depth-32 call descent it is exponential and hung
// test_firmware_patterns for >4 minutes to buy this one form. The summary is O(F):
// measured 2.5s for that same test.
//
// NEGATIVE-only by necessity: gcc refuses __attribute__((interrupt)) on hosted
// x86-64, so this cannot be a running positive. It fails at the CHECKER, before gcc.
volatile u32 g;

void bump(volatile *u32 p) { *p += 1; }

interrupt TIM2 { g = 1; }

u32 main() { bump(&g); return 0; }
